+  </p>
+  <p class="post-category">The Sunday Letter &middot; Issue 07</p>
+
+  <h1 class="post-h1">Three Myths That Are Costing<br>You Money and <em>Sleep</em></h1>
+
+  <div class="post-meta-row">
+    <span class="post-meta-author">Lauren Fine, MD</span>
+    <span class="post-meta-dot"></span>
+    <span>October 18, 2026</span>
+    <span class="post-meta-dot"></span>
+    <span>6 min read</span>
+  </div>
+</div>
+
+<article class="post-body">
+
+  <p>Before I was a coach, I was an allergist. I still am. I fell for allergy as a summer research student at Children’s Hospital Cincinnati, watching how much the kids in clinic suffered. Years later I found I loved teaching as much as my own clinic, and I made a change.</p>
+  <p>And I keep noticing that the myths I correct as an allergist look a lot like the ones I hear as a coach.</p>
+  <p>This week, three of them. Each one has a bedroom version that can save you money. Each one has a life version that can save you a lot more.</p>
+
+  <h2>Three myths</h2>
+
+  <h3>Myth 1: “If it's worn out, replace the whole thing”</h3>
+  <p><strong>In your bedroom</strong></p>
+  <p>You have seen the ads. Your mattress is full of dust mites, so replace it every 7 to 10 years. That rule has been promoted for years by the mattress industry's own trade group. It sells a lot of mattresses.</p>
+  <p>For dust mites, there is a much cheaper answer. A <strong>zippered</strong> encasement. A fitted cover leaves the bottom open. A zippered one seals all six sides. The mites already inside are trapped. They can't reach you. New mites and bed bugs stay out.</p>
+  <p>A $2,000 mattress on an 8-year cycle costs you $250 a year. A good encasement often costs less than $100, once. If your mattress is still comfortable, keep it.</p>
+  <p><strong>In your life</strong></p>
+  <p>The same reflex shows up when work starts to feel wrong. Quit. Start over. Replace the whole thing.</p>
+  <p>When I moved from clinic to teaching, my one hesitation was leaving the thing I had always thought I was supposed to do for the thing I discovered was missing. What I didn’t realize right away was that my teaching makes me a better clinician (still one day a week 🥰), and that my clinical work would make me a better teacher. Neither could be what they are now on their own, and neither could continue to grow without the other.</p>
+  <p>Here is the myth: to make a change in our life, we have to completely start over. That's not necessarily the right answer. Much of what we learned along the way can and should be brought with us when we make a change. It is what makes the new path even better and stronger than if we hadn't lived through everything else first.</p>
+  <p>Protect what still works before you pay to replace it.</p>
+  <p><em>Ask yourself: What have I learned along the way that I want to bring with me?</em></p>
+  <p><a href="/dust-mite-allergy"><strong>Read: The allergist's bedroom checklist</strong></a></p>
+
+  <h3>Myth 2: “The label tells you what's inside”</h3>
+  <p><strong>In your bathroom</strong></p>
+  <p>In the United States, the word hypoallergenic has no legal definition. The FDA says it means whatever a company wants it to mean. No test. No proof.</p>
+  <p>When researchers checked 187 children's products labeled hypoallergenic, fragrance free or dermatologist tested, 89 percent contained a known allergen. Skip the word on the front. Look for fragrance free, then read the list on the back.</p>
+  <p><strong>In your life</strong></p>
+  <p>We wear labels too. Successful. Reliable. The one who always says yes. A title or a role can sound reassuring from the outside. It tells you nothing about what is actually in your day, or whether it agrees with you.</p>
+  <p>Read the list, not the label. Look at where your hours really go.</p>
+  <p><em>Ask yourself: If I read the ingredient list of my week, what would surprise me?</em></p>
+  <p><a href="/hypoallergenic-skin-care"><strong>Read: Does hypoallergenic mean allergy free?</strong></a></p>
+
+  <h3>Myth 3: “One big purchase will fix it”</h3>
+  <p><strong>In your home</strong></p>
+  <p>An air purifier won't fix a dust mite allergy on its own. Mite allergen rides on heavy particles that settle within minutes. It lives in your bedding, carpet and couch, not in the air.</p>
+  <p>What works is the boring stuff, done together. Encase the bed. Wash bedding weekly in water at least 130 degrees. Keep humidity under 50 percent. Vacuum with a sealed HEPA vacuum. A purifier is still worth having for pets or pollen. Just don't let it be the only thing you buy.</p>
+  <p><strong>In your life</strong></p>
+  <p>The life version is the big fix. The vacation. The retreat. The new planner. The new job. Each one can help. None of them works alone.</p>
+  <p>The change that lasts is usually small, repeated and a little boring. A few habits, done in order, for long enough to notice.</p>
+  <p><em>Ask yourself: What small thing could I do every week instead of waiting for the big fix?</em></p>
+
+  <div class="sl-panel">
+    <p class="sl-panel-label">What to do with it</p>
+    <p>Pick one myth. The one you have already spent money, or years, on.</p>
+    <p>Make one change in each version this week.</p>
+    <p><strong>Zip the mattress,</strong> and protect one evening.</p>
+    <p><strong>Swap one scented product,</strong> and drop one thing you only do because of a label.</p>
+    <p><strong>Wash the sheets hot on Sunday,</strong> and pick one small habit to repeat.</p>
+    <p>Try it for two weeks and notice how you sleep.</p>
+  </div>
+
+  <div class="sl-coach">
+    <p class="sl-panel-label">The coach's view</p>
+    <p>Every one of these myths sells the same thing. A rule you don't have to examine. Replace it on schedule. Trust the word on the front. Buy the big fix.</p>
+    <p style="margin-top:18px;">Rules like that save us time. They also cost us money, sleep and sometimes years. The fix is rarely dramatic. It is noticing the rule, checking it, and deciding whether it still fits.</p>
+    <p style="margin-top:18px;"><strong style="color:#fff;">This week's question:</strong> What rule are you following that nobody ever made you check?</p>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2 id="worth-your-time">Worth your time</h2>
+
+  <div class="sl-picks">
+    <div class="sl-pick">
+      <p class="sl-pick-num">01 &middot; Listen</p>
+      <h3 class="sl-pick-title">House Dust Mite Allergy Explained: Symptoms, Triggers, and Practical Solutions</h3>
+      <p>Hope &amp; Histamine: The Paediatric Allergy Podcast. A clear, 34-minute walk through where dust mites live, how they drive year-round symptoms, eczema and asthma, how the allergy is diagnosed, and what actually reduces exposure at home.</p>
+      <p><strong>My take:</strong> <em>The podcast is very informative, created through Google notebook, appears to be based on three cited articles.</em></p>
+      <a class="sl-pick-link" href="https://hopeandhistaminepodcasts.buzzsprout.com/2509328/episodes/18437631-house-dust-mite-allergy-explained-symptoms-triggers-and-practical-solutions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Listen to the episode</a>
+    </div>
+    <div class="sl-pick">
+      <p class="sl-pick-num">02 &middot; Read</p>
+      <h3 class="sl-pick-title">"Hypoallergenic" and "Fragrance-Free" Moisturizer Claims Are Often False</h3>
+      <p>NPR, on a JAMA Dermatology study of the 100 best-selling moisturizers. 83 percent of those labeled hypoallergenic contained a potential allergen, and "dermatologist recommended" turned out to mean very little.</p>
+      <p><strong>My take:</strong> <em>It is important to be educated on what companies are required to disclose in their products, and how labels can be misleading. This could potentially make a big difference in finding the right product for you and your family.</em></p>
+      <a class="sl-pick-link" href="https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2017/10/02/554365324/hypoallergenic-and-fragrance-free-moisturizer-claims-are-often-false" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the article</a>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+
