+  <p>Holidays are a good kind of busy for us. We tend to use this time to gather with family and relax. What I love about it is that we have the opportunity to see almost everyone in our family, with a few exceptions. Our rule for holidays is that everyone is welcome, even friends and acquaintances. It is such a tough time to be alone or not have plans, so our home is always open to anyone who needs to feel a part of something. No questions asked, no explanation needed. ❤️</p>
+  <p>The holidays are almost here. Before the boxes come down from the attic, this week is about what we unpack without checking. At home, and in our lives.</p>
+
+  <h2>The holidays</h2>
+
+  <h3>In your home</h3>
+  <p>I have noticed that so many people are craving calm and quiet during the holidays. It feels like since COVID this has been more of a tendency than before. Anyone else notice this?</p>
+  <p>Holiday allergies often hide in the decorations. Four places to check:</p>
+  <p><strong>The live tree. </strong>Researchers at SUNY Upstate Medical University found 53 molds on clippings from 28 live Christmas trees, including aspergillus, penicillium, cladosporium and alternaria, the kinds most likely to trigger allergies. The American College of Allergy, Asthma and Immunology suggests rinsing a live tree before you bring it inside. A true HEPA air purifier in the same room helps with what still comes off it. My allergist pick is the <a href="https://go.shopmy.us/p-88239280" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">Blueair Classic Pro</a>, which is asthma and allergy friendly certified.</p>
+  <p><strong>The boxes. </strong>Artificial trees and ornaments collect dust and mold in storage from year to year. Clean them before they go up, ideally outside or in the garage. Use a vacuum with a sealed HEPA system, like the <a href="https://go.shopmy.us/p-88239577" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">Kenmore Elite Pet Friendly Upright</a>, so the dust stays in the vacuum. If your boxes live in a damp basement or garage, a <a href="https://go.shopmy.us/p-82289371" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">dehumidifier</a> keeps mold from moving in before next year.</p>
+  <p><strong>The candles. </strong>The ACAAI notes that scented candles can quickly trigger allergy and asthma symptoms. Flameless candles give you the look without the scent or smoke. <a href="https://go.shopmy.us/p-97407805" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">Luminara</a> makes unscented wax ones with a realistic flame.</p>
+  <p><strong>The visit. </strong>Other homes can have hidden dust, mold and pet dander, even when they look spotless. Pack your allergy medicine before you need it. If you are staying over, zippered <a href="https://go.shopmy.us/p-82283141" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">pillow protectors</a> fold flat in a suitcase and seal out dust mites and dander.</p>
+
+  <h3>In your life</h3>
+  <p>We do the same thing with the season itself. We bring last year out of the box and put it straight back up. The same hosting, the same gifts for the same people, the same schedule, the same promise that this year we will rest.</p>
+  <p>Most of us are not imagining the strain. In an American Psychological Association poll from November 2023, 89 percent of U.S. adults said something causes them stress during the holiday season, and 43 percent said it gets in the way of enjoying it. The top causes were money (58 percent), finding the right gifts (40 percent) and missing loved ones (38 percent).</p>
+  <p>It's not something we stopped doing, but something we love doing. Slowing down, doing things we don't do the rest of the year. Go to the mall especially on off days, or meet at the outlet mall halfway between us and some of our family that lives a little further away than they used to and for this reason we don't see them as often. I can say that what we don't do is treat the holidays as something that needs to be perfect or that we need to plan fully. Approaching them as a time to unwind, be with family and friends, but not try to do too much or be perfect.</p>
+  <p>The fix is the same as the ornaments. Check it before it goes up. Decide now, in October, what you are bringing out this year. It is much harder to set a boundary at the Thanksgiving table than three weeks before it.</p>
+  <p>It helps to see it on paper. Open a <a href="https://go.shopmy.us/p-93292782" target="_blank" rel="noopener sponsored">weekly planner</a> and write in what you are keeping before anyone else fills the weeks.</p>
+  <p><em>Ask yourself: What am I about to unpack this year that I never actually chose?</em></p>
+
+  <div class="sl-panel">
+    <p class="sl-panel-label">What to do with it</p>
+    <p>Three small things this week.</p>
+    <p><strong>Wipe down the decoration boxes</strong> before they come into the house.</p>
+    <p><strong>Pick one holiday tradition</strong> and ask whether it still fits. Keep it, change it or let it go.</p>
+    <p><strong>Say one no now,</strong> before the calendar fills. One event, one gift exchange or one dish you do not have to make.</p>
+  </div>
+
+  <div class="sl-coach">
+    <p class="sl-panel-label">The coach's view</p>
+    <p>The holidays come with a box of rules we packed away last January. Host because we always host. Buy because we always buy. Say yes because it is the season.</p>
+    <p style="margin-top:18px;">None of them are wrong. They are just unchecked. A season you chose feels very different from one you inherited.</p>
+    <p style="margin-top:18px;"><strong style="color:#fff;">This week's question:</strong> What would make this the first holiday season you actually planned for yourself?</p>
+  </div>
