+      "name": "How long does it take to form a habit?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "Longer than most people expect. In a 2010 University College London study led by Phillippa Lally, the typical time for a daily behavior to become automatic was about 66 days. Individual times ranged from 18 days to an estimated 254 days."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "Is the 21-day rule true?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "No. The idea traces back to plastic surgeon Maxwell Maltz, who wrote in his 1960 book Psycho-Cybernetics that it took a minimum of about 21 days for an old self-image to change. Over time that became a promise that habits form in 21 days. The research does not support it."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "What is habit stacking?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "Habit stacking means attaching a new habit to one you already do every day, using the formula: after I do my current habit, I will do my new habit. For example, after I pour my morning coffee, I will write one line in a notebook."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "Why can't I stick to habits?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "Usually the habit is too big, it has no set time or place, or nothing came off your plate to make room for it. Shrink it, tie it to a cue you already have, and decide what it replaces."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "Does missing a day ruin a habit?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "No. In the Lally study, missing one opportunity did not materially affect habit formation. Many missed days did slow progress, so the practical rule is to get back on track the next day."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "What is the easiest way to start a new habit?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "Pick one small habit, decide exactly when and where you will do it, and try it for two weeks. Two weeks will not make it automatic. It gives you enough information to decide whether to keep going."
+      }
+    }
