+
+  <h1 class="post-h1">How to Keep Your New Year's Resolutions <em>Past January</em></h1>
+
+  <p class="post-script">one goal, made small, given room</p>
+
+  <div class="post-meta-row">
+    <span class="post-meta-author">Lauren Fine, MD</span>
+    <span class="post-meta-dot"></span>
+    <span>November 1, 2026</span>
+    <span class="post-meta-dot"></span>
+    <span>9 min read</span>
+  </div>
+</div>
+
+<!-- POST BODY -->
+<article class="post-body">
+  <p>Every January, millions of people make the same promise. Exercise more. Eat better. Save money. Be calmer. By February, most of those promises have gone quiet. Nobody announces it. The gym bag just stays in the car.</p>
+  <p>If that has happened to you, you are not lazy and you are not broken. You probably set a goal the way most of us were taught to set goals. Big, vague, and added on top of a life that was already full.</p>
+  <p>This guide covers why resolutions fail, what the research says about the ones that last, and a simple plan for setting goals you will actually keep. It starts with a question most resolution advice skips. Where is the time going to come from?</p>
+
+  <h2>Why do New Year's resolutions fail?</h2>
+  <p>Most resolutions fail for predictable reasons. Once you can name them, you can plan around them.</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>They are too big.</strong> "Get in shape" is a direction. It gives you nothing to do on a Tuesday night.</li>
+    <li><strong>They are framed as stopping.</strong> "Stop eating sugar" and "quit scrolling" leave a hole and nothing to fill it with.</li>
+    <li><strong>They have no when or where.</strong> A goal without a time and place competes with everything else on your calendar, and it usually loses.</li>
+    <li><strong>They are all or nothing.</strong> One missed workout feels like failure, so the whole plan gets abandoned.</li>
+    <li><strong>Nothing comes off the calendar.</strong> This is the big one. You cannot add a new habit to a full life without taking something out.</li>
+  </ul>
+  <p>That last reason matters most for high achievers. They rarely lack discipline. They lack room. Their weeks are already packed with work, family, and everyone else's requests. A resolution gets squeezed in at 5 a.m. or after the kids are asleep, and it lasts until the first bad week.</p>
+  <p>In medicine, we do not tell a patient to "be healthier" and send them home. We write a plan. What to do, how much, when, and when we will check in. Your goals deserve the same treatment.</p>
+
+  <h2>What percentage of people keep their New Year's resolutions?</h2>
+  <p>You have probably seen a scary number online claiming that almost everyone fails. The research is more encouraging, and more useful.</p>
+  <p>Start with who makes them. <a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2024/01/29/new-years-resolutions-who-makes-them-and-why/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A 2024 Pew Research Center survey</a> found that 30% of U.S. adults made at least one resolution for that year. Most were about health, exercise, or diet. The survey ran in mid-January, so it captured people only a few weeks in. At that point, 59% said they had kept all of them.</p>
+  <p>The longer studies tell you more. <a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/jclp.1151" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A 2002 study in the Journal of Clinical Psychology</a> followed 159 people who made resolutions for six months. At the six-month mark, 46% were still succeeding.</p>
+  <p><a href="https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0234097" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A 2020 study in PLOS ONE</a> followed more than 1,000 people for a full year. When it ended, 55% considered themselves successful.</p>
+  <p>So roughly half of people who make a resolution are still on track months later. That is not a guarantee. It is also far better than "almost everyone fails." Your odds are real, and how you set the goal changes them.</p>
+
+  <h2>Is it even worth making a resolution?</h2>
+  <p>Yes. The same 2002 study compared resolvers with 123 people who wanted to make a similar change but did not make a resolution. At six months, 46% of resolvers were succeeding. Only 4% of the non-resolvers were.</p>
+  <p>Deciding on purpose matters. Writing it down matters. Picking a start date matters.</p>
+  <p>There is also a reason January feels like the right time. <a href="https://faculty.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2014/06/Dai_Fresh_Start_2014_Mgmt_Sci.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Researchers Hengchen Dai, Katherine Milkman and Jason Riis</a> called it the "fresh start effect." In their 2014 paper in Management Science, Google searches for "diet" rose at the start of each new year. Gym visits rose at the start of a new week, a new month, and after birthdays. A clean page on the calendar helps people separate from their old habits and try again.</p>
+  <p>That is good news for two reasons. January is a useful start. And it is not the only one.</p>
+
+  <h2>How to set goals you'll actually keep</h2>
+  <p>Before you write anything down, pick one goal. One. Every new goal needs time, attention and energy, and those come from the same limited supply. Five resolutions split that supply five ways.</p>
+
+  <h3>1. Aim toward something, not away from something</h3>
+  <p>The 2020 PLOS ONE study tested this directly. People with approach goals (adding something, like "eat a vegetable with lunch") were more successful than people with avoidance goals (cutting something, like "stop snacking"). At one year, 58.9% of approach goals succeeded, compared with 47.1% of avoidance goals.</p>
+  <p>So turn your "stop" into a "start." "Quit my phone at night" becomes "read for ten minutes in bed." "Stop skipping lunch" becomes "eat lunch away from my desk on Mondays and Thursdays."</p>
+
+  <h3>2. Make it smaller than your ambition</h3>
+  <p>Your ambition says run a marathon. Your first goal says walk for ten minutes after dinner. Start at a size you could keep on your worst week, not your best one. You can always add more once the habit holds. I cover this in more detail in <a href="/how-to-build-a-habit">how to build a habit that lasts</a>.</p>
+
+  <h3>3. Decide exactly when and where</h3>
+  <p>Psychologists call this an implementation intention. It is a simple sentence: "When X happens, I will do Y." <a href="https://kops.uni-konstanz.de/entities/publication/2e749bfb-8533-437c-8203-7e788c910c5f" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A 2006 meta-analysis by Peter Gollwitzer and Paschal Sheeran</a> pooled 94 tests of this technique and found a medium-to-large effect on reaching goals.</p>
+  <p>Some examples:</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li>"When I pour my morning coffee, I will write one line in my journal."</li>
+    <li>"When I park at work, I will take the stairs."</li>
+    <li>"When I close my laptop for the day, I will put my phone in the kitchen."</li>
+  </ul>
+  <p>The "when" does the remembering for you. You stop relying on motivation to show up at the right moment.</p>
+
+  <h3>4. Name what it replaces</h3>
+  <p>This is the step that makes room. Before you add anything, decide what comes out. Thirty minutes of scrolling. One standing meeting you do not need. A volunteer role you said yes to out of guilt.</p>
+  <p>If you are not sure where your time goes, the <a href="/sunday-letter-09-what-will-you-do-with-the-extra-hour">Find Your Hour worksheet</a> walks you through a quick time audit. If the problem is that you keep saying yes, start with <a href="/how-to-say-no">how to say no</a> and <a href="/how-to-set-boundaries">how to set boundaries</a>. Often the real resolution underneath the resolution is to <a href="/how-to-stop-people-pleasing">stop people-pleasing</a> so there is something left for you.</p>
+
+  <!-- LAUREN: add your own example of a goal you kept or dropped here, and what you had to take off your plate to make room for it -->
+
+  <h2>How do you stick to your goals after the first few weeks?</h2>
+  <p>Setting the goal is the easy part. The hard part starts around the third week, when the novelty fades and real life pushes back. These are the habits that carry a goal through that stretch.</p>
+
+  <h3>Try it for two weeks</h3>
+  <p>Do not commit to forever. Commit to two weeks. Two weeks is short enough to start without dread and long enough to learn something. At the end, ask three questions. Did I do it? Did it help? What needs to change? Then sign up for two more.</p>
+
+  <h3>Expect it to take longer than 21 days</h3>
+  <p>The idea that a habit forms in 21 days is not backed by good data. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/ejsp.674" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A 2010 study in the European Journal of Social Psychology</a> led by Phillippa Lally tracked 96 volunteers building a new daily habit. On average, it took 66 days for the behavior to feel automatic. The range was huge, from 18 days to an estimated 254.</p>
+  <p>So if it still feels like effort in February, nothing is wrong with you. You are in the middle, right on schedule.</p>
+
+  <h3>Miss once, not twice</h3>
+  <p>The same study found that missing a single day had little effect on building the habit. One miss is normal. What hurts is letting one miss turn into a week, then a month. Make a rule that you never miss twice in a row.</p>
+
+  <h3>Lower the bar on hard days</h3>
+  <p>On a terrible day, do the smallest version. Five minutes instead of thirty. One page instead of a chapter. The point is to keep the habit alive until the next good day.</p>
+
+  <h3>Tell one person</h3>
+  <p>In the 2020 PLOS ONE study, the group that got some support did better than the group that got none. More structure was not better, though. The group that got the most instructions and goal-setting exercises did no better than the group with no support at all. A little encouragement went further than a lot of homework. Pick one person to tell and check in with them once a week.</p>
+
+  <blockquote>
+    <p>Most people do not need more willpower. They need more room.</p>
+  </blockquote>
+
+  <h2>What should you do if you've already broken your resolution?</h2>
+  <p>Start again. Then start smaller.</p>
+  <p>A broken resolution is information. Maybe the goal was too big. Maybe the time you picked never worked. Maybe it was a goal you thought you were supposed to want. Each of those tells you something useful about the next version.</p>
+  <p>I think of it like a compost pile. The effort you already put in does not disappear when a plan falls apart. It goes in the pile, breaks down, and feeds what you grow next. The three weeks of workouts in January still taught you which time of day works. The budget you abandoned still showed you where the money goes. Nothing you tried was wasted.</p>
+  <p>Remember the fresh start effect. You do not need to wait until next January. Next Monday works. So does the first of the month or your birthday. Pick a new start date, shrink the goal, and try it for two weeks.</p>
+  <p>And if the resolution fell apart because your whole life feels overloaded, the problem may be bigger than one goal. My guide to <a href="/work-life-balance">work-life balance</a> and my guide to <a href="/how-to-make-time-for-yourself">making time for yourself</a> are good places to look at the bigger picture.</p>
+
+  <!-- LAUREN: add a short true example of a time you restarted a goal after dropping it, if you want one here -->
+
+  <h2>A simple plan for this year's resolution</h2>
+  <p>If you want the whole page in one list, here it is.</p>
+  <ol>
+    <li><strong>Pick one goal.</strong> The one that would make the biggest difference to how your days feel.</li>
+    <li><strong>Turn it into a "start."</strong> Something you add, not something you cut.</li>
+    <li><strong>Shrink it.</strong> A version you could keep on your worst week.</li>
+    <li><strong>Write your when-then sentence.</strong> "When X happens, I will do Y."</li>
+    <li><strong>Name what it replaces.</strong> Take something off your calendar to make room.</li>
+    <li><strong>Tell one person.</strong> Check in once a week.</li>
+    <li><strong>Try it for two weeks.</strong> Then review, adjust, and go again.</li>
+  </ol>
+  <p>If your goal is about health, <a href="/make-time-for-healthy-habits">how to make time for healthy habits</a> applies this same approach to exercise and cooking. For more worksheets like Find Your Hour, browse the <a href="/resources">free resources</a>.</p>
+
+  <h2>Make room</h2>
+  <p>A resolution is a decision about what matters to you this year. That decision only holds if your calendar agrees with it. So make the goal small, give it a time and place, and take something out to make room for it. Then try it for two weeks.</p>
+  <p>I write more about making room in <a href="/sunday-letter">The Sunday Letter</a> and on <a href="https://permissiontochange.substack.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">my Substack, Permission to Change</a>. If you want help deciding which goal matters most and what has to come off your plate, you can <a href="/free-session">book a free 55-minute session</a>. No sales pitch. You can also read more about how <a href="/coaching">coaching</a> works.</p>
+
+  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
+  <h3>What percentage of people keep their New Year's resolutions?</h3>
+  <p>It depends on the study and how long you wait. In a 2002 study in the Journal of Clinical Psychology, 46% of resolvers were still succeeding at six months. In a 2020 study in PLOS ONE, 55% of participants considered themselves successful after a full year.</p>
+  <h3>Why do most New Year's resolutions fail?</h3>
+  <p>Most fail because they are too big, too vague, framed as something to stop rather than something to start, and added to a calendar that was already full. Nothing was taken off to make room.</p>
+  <h3>Are New Year's resolutions worth making?</h3>
+  <p>Yes. In the 2002 Journal of Clinical Psychology study, people who made a resolution were far more likely to succeed at six months than people with similar goals who did not, 46% compared with 4%.</p>
+  <h3>How long does it take for a new habit to stick?</h3>
+  <p>Longer than 21 days. A 2010 study in the European Journal of Social Psychology found it took an average of 66 days for a new behavior to become automatic, with a wide range from 18 to 254 days.</p>
+  <h3>What should I do if I already broke my resolution?</h3>
+  <p>Start again, smaller. Missing one day has little effect on building a habit. Keep what you learned, shrink the goal, pick a new start date such as next Monday, and try it for two weeks.</p>
+  <h3>How many resolutions should I make?</h3>
+  <p>One is enough. Every new goal needs time, and that time has to come from somewhere. Pick the one that matters most, make room for it, and add another only after the first one holds.</p>
+</article>
