+
+  <h1 class="post-h1">How to Make Time for Yourself When <em>Everyone Needs You</em></h1>
+
+  <p class="post-script">one hour that belongs to you</p>
+
+  <div class="post-meta-row">
+    <span class="post-meta-author">Lauren Fine, MD</span>
+    <span class="post-meta-dot"></span>
+    <span>November 1, 2026</span>
+    <span class="post-meta-dot"></span>
+    <span>9 min read</span>
+  </div>
+</div>
+
+<!-- POST BODY -->
+<article class="post-body">
+  <p>You take care of patients, clients, kids, parents, a team, a house. Everyone gets a piece of your day. You get whatever is left, and most days nothing is left.</p>
+  <p>If you have typed "no time for myself" into a search bar at 11 p.m., you are in good company. You will never find time for yourself. You have to make room for it. This guide shows you how, starting with one hour a week.</p>
+
+  <h2>Why do I have no time for myself?</h2>
+  <p>Because time for yourself is the only thing on your calendar that nobody else is waiting for. Work has deadlines. Kids have pickup times. Other people ask out loud. You do not ask, so you do not get.</p>
+  <p>The feeling is common, and it is sharpest for the people carrying the most. In a <a href="https://news.gallup.com/poll/187982/americans-perceived-time-crunch-no-worse-past.aspx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">December 2015 Gallup poll</a>, 48% of U.S. adults said they did not have enough time to do what they wanted. Among working Americans the number was 61%. Among parents of children under 18, it was also 61%.</p>
+  <p>Researchers call this time poverty: the ongoing sense that there is never enough time. It is a real strain, and it does not get better by waiting for a calm week. The calm week does not come.</p>
+
+  <h2>Is it selfish to make time for yourself?</h2>
+  <p>No. Time for yourself is maintenance. No physician would tell a patient to skip sleep, skip meals and run on empty for months, then act surprised when something breaks. Yet many of us do exactly that to ourselves and call it dedication.</p>
+  <p>The people who depend on you get a better version of you when you have had some room to breathe. More patient. More present. Less likely to snap over a lost shoe. Making time for yourself is part of how you keep showing up for them.</p>
+  <p>Here is the deeper point. Many of us try to make up for a life with no room in it. We plan a big vacation. We buy something nice. Those help for a week. Then we come home to the same calendar. The better fix is to change how the ordinary weeks are built, so you find some joy in them instead of compensating for them later. That is what this page is about.</p>
+
+  <div class="reel">
+    <video controls playsinline preload="none" poster="/reel-not-bad-at-time-management.jpg" aria-label="Video: You're not bad at time management">
+      <source src="/reel-not-bad-at-time-management.mp4" type="video/mp4">
+    </video>
+    <div>
+      <p class="reel-kicker">Watch · 41 seconds</p>
+      <p class="reel-title">You're not bad at time management</p>
+      <p class="reel-text">Sometimes you're carrying a list that was never realistically possible. Question the diagnosis, then learn how to say no to what doesn't belong on it.</p>
+      <p class="reel-more"><a href="/watch">More short videos</a></p>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2>How do I find out where my time goes?</h2>
+  <p>Before you can make room, you need to see what is filling the space. Most of us guess wrong. We remember the big obligations and forget the twenty minutes here and there that disappear into email, errands and the phone.</p>
+  <p>Start with a time audit. I built a free, ten-minute version called <a href="/sunday-letter-09-what-will-you-do-with-the-extra-hour#find-your-hour"><strong>Find Your Hour</strong></a>. It is adapted from the time audit Harvard Business School professor Ashley Whillans uses in her research. It has five steps:</p>
+  <ol>
+    <li><strong>Map a normal day.</strong> Pick a regular Tuesday and write down what fills it. Include the scrolling.</li>
+    <li><strong>Mark each one.</strong> M for meaningful, P for pleasant, D for draining. Some things get two letters.</li>
+    <li><strong>Find the leaks.</strong> Circle anything marked only D. Ask: can I stop it, hand it off, or pay to have it done?</li>
+    <li><strong>Claim one hour.</strong> Pick one hour this week you will not give away. Put it on your calendar now.</li>
+    <li><strong>Decide what it is for.</strong> Sleep, a walk, a friend, a book or nothing at all. It only has to be yours.</li>
+  </ol>
+  <p><a href="/sunday-letter-09-what-will-you-do-with-the-extra-hour#find-your-hour">Get the full Find Your Hour worksheet here</a>. It takes ten minutes. Most people find at least one hour they did not know they had. You can find more free worksheets on the <a href="/resources">resources page</a>.</p>
+
+  <h2>How do you find time for yourself as a working mom?</h2>
+  <p>Working mothers carry two full jobs and a mental load that never clocks out. The usual self care advice (a bubble bath, a spa day) misses the point. A bath does not help if you spend it thinking about the permission slip you forgot to sign.</p>
+  <p>Self care for busy moms has to be built into the week, not squeezed in after it. A few things that help:</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>Schedule it like an appointment.</strong> If it is not on the calendar, it is optional, and optional things lose to everyone else's needs.</li>
+    <li><strong>Trade time with your partner.</strong> You take Saturday morning, they take Sunday morning. Name it out loud so it is protected.</li>
+    <li><strong>Lower the standard on something.</strong> Store-bought cupcakes for the class party. Laundry folded once a week. Pick one place to be good enough instead of perfect.</li>
+    <li><strong>Use the margins on purpose.</strong> The fifteen minutes before school pickup can be a podcast you chose, not more email.</li>
+    <li><strong>Let the kids see you take it.</strong> They learn what a full life looks like by watching yours.</li>
+  </ul>
+  <!-- LAUREN: add your own example here (how you protect time for yourself as a physician and mom) -->
+  <p>If the problem is that every request lands on you, the fix starts with boundaries. Read <a href="/how-to-set-boundaries">how to set boundaries</a> when you are the one everyone relies on.</p>
+
+  <h2>How do I have me time when my day is in pieces?</h2>
+  <p>Sometimes you do have free time. It just comes in scraps. Five minutes in the car line. Ten minutes between meetings. Twenty minutes after the kids are asleep, half of it spent answering texts.</p>
+  <p>Ashley Whillans calls this <a href="https://behavioralscientist.org/time-confetti-and-the-broken-promise-of-leisure/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">time confetti</a>, a term coined by journalist Brigid Schulte. In her book <em>Time Smart</em> (Harvard Business Review Press, 2020), Whillans describes it as little bits of seconds and minutes lost to unproductive multitasking. She points to phones and constant notifications as a main cause. An hour of free time broken up by pings does not feel like an hour.</p>
+  <p>So aim for time in one piece. Try this:</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>Protect one block.</strong> Thirty uninterrupted minutes beats an hour of scraps.</li>
+    <li><strong>Put the phone in another room.</strong> Notifications off, door closed, if you can.</li>
+    <li><strong>Batch the small stuff.</strong> Answer texts and email at set times instead of all evening long.</li>
+    <li><strong>Pick in advance.</strong> Decide what the block is for before it starts, so you do not drift into scrolling.</li>
+  </ul>
+
+  <h2>Should I pay to get time back?</h2>
+  <p>If you can afford it, the research says yes. In a 2017 study in <em>PNAS</em>, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1706541114" target="_blank" rel="noopener">"Buying time promotes happiness"</a>, Whillans and colleagues at Harvard Business School and the University of British Columbia surveyed more than 6,000 adults in the United States, Canada, Denmark and the Netherlands. People who spent money to buy themselves free time reported greater life satisfaction, and that link held even after accounting for income.</p>
+  <p>They also ran an experiment. Sixty working adults spent $40 on a time-saving purchase one weekend and $40 on a material purchase another weekend. They felt happier after the time-saving purchase.</p>
+  <p>Most of us do not think this way. In the same paper, only 2% of 98 working adults said they would spend a $40 windfall on something that saved them time. Even among 850 millionaires surveyed, almost half spent nothing to outsource tasks they disliked.</p>
+  <p>Paying for time can be small. Grocery delivery. A cleaner every other week. A teenager who walks the dog. Then use the time you buy for something that fills you up, not for more work. And if paying is not an option, look at the other two questions from the audit: can you stop it, or can you hand it off?</p>
+
+  <h2>How do you find time for hobbies?</h2>
+  <p>Hobbies are often the first thing high achievers give up. They feel indulgent. They do not produce anything you can put on a CV. That is part of why they matter. A hobby is one of the few places where you get to be a beginner, with no stakes and no one grading you.</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li><strong>Start smaller than you think you should.</strong> Twenty minutes of the guitar, one chapter of a novel, one row of knitting.</li>
+    <li><strong>Attach it to an anchor.</strong> Read after the kids go to bed. Sketch with your morning coffee. New habits stick better when they ride on old ones. More on that in <a href="/how-to-build-a-habit">how to build a habit</a>.</li>
+    <li><strong>Make it social.</strong> A class, a walking group or a book club puts it on the calendar and gives you a reason to show up.</li>
+    <li><strong>Make room first.</strong> Use the <a href="/sunday-letter-04-make-room">one in, one out rule</a>. Before you add a hobby, name what it replaces.</li>
+  </ul>
+  <!-- LAUREN: add your own example here (a hobby you made room for, or one you want to get back to) -->
+  <p>If you have tried this before and it faded by February, read <a href="/how-to-keep-new-years-resolutions">how to keep New Year's resolutions</a> past the first few weeks. The same rules apply to a hobby.</p>
+
+  <h2>What if I feel guilty taking time for myself?</h2>
+  <p>Guilt usually means you have been taught that your needs come last. It does not mean you are doing something wrong. Notice the guilt, name it, and take the hour anyway.</p>
+  <p>For many women, the guilt comes from a long habit of keeping everyone else comfortable. If you find yourself giving your hour away the moment someone asks, read <a href="/how-to-stop-people-pleasing">how to stop people pleasing</a>. Then practice one sentence: "I'm not available then." You do not owe a reason. If you want more words, <a href="/how-to-say-no">these scripts for saying no</a> work at the office and at home.</p>
+  <!-- LAUREN: add your own example here (a moment you felt guilty taking time for yourself and what you did) -->
+  <p>It also helps to remember the bigger picture. <a href="/work-life-balance">Work-life balance</a> is not a perfect split. It is a life where the things that matter to you, including you, get real time on the calendar.</p>
+
+  <h2>Make room</h2>
+  <p>You do not need a free weekend or a quiet month. You need one hour that belongs to you, protected like any other appointment. Do the <a href="/sunday-letter-09-what-will-you-do-with-the-extra-hour#find-your-hour">Find Your Hour</a> audit. Claim the hour. Try it for two weeks and notice what changes.</p>
+  <p>If you are also trying to fit in exercise or better meals, the same approach works there. See <a href="/make-time-for-healthy-habits">how to make time for healthy habits</a>. I write about making room every week on <a href="https://permissiontochange.substack.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">my Substack, Permission to Change</a>.</p>
+  <p>And if you want help figuring out what to take off your plate, <a href="/free-session">book a free 55-minute session</a>. No sales pitch. You can also read more about how <a href="/coaching">coaching</a> works.</p>
+
+  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
+  <h3>How do I make time for myself when I'm busy?</h3>
+  <p>Start with a time audit to see where your hours go, then claim one hour a week and put it on your calendar like an appointment. Protect it for two weeks before you add more.</p>
+  <h3>How much time for myself do I need?</h3>
+  <p>There is no magic number. One protected hour a week is a realistic start for most busy people. Uninterrupted time matters more than total minutes, so aim for one solid block instead of scattered scraps.</p>
+  <h3>How can a working mom find time for herself?</h3>
+  <p>Schedule it, trade protected time with a partner, lower the standard on one household task, and outsource what you can afford to. Build self care into the week instead of waiting for leftover time.</p>
+  <h3>Is it selfish to make time for yourself?</h3>
+  <p>No. Time for yourself is maintenance. You show up with more patience and energy for the people who depend on you when you are not running on empty.</p>
+  <h3>How do I find time for hobbies?</h3>
+  <p>Start with twenty minutes, attach it to something you already do, and make it social if you can. Before you add it, decide what it replaces on your calendar.</p>
+  <h3>What is time confetti?</h3>
+  <p>Time confetti is a term coined by journalist Brigid Schulte and used by Harvard Business School professor Ashley Whillans for the small bits of seconds and minutes lost to unproductive multitasking. Phones and notifications break free time into pieces that do not feel restful.</p>
+</article>
